1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:04,908
Here's what most competing pitches sound like: guaranteed results, a portfolio of

2
00:00:04,908 --> 00:00:09,579
nothing but winners, promises about what will "go viral." None of that is a system.

3
00:00:09,579 --> 00:00:14,306
It's a hope dressed up as a plan, and anyone who's run ads for more than a month has

4
00:00:14,306 --> 00:00:17,401
already been burned by exactly this kind of pitch once.

5
00:00:17,401 --> 00:00:21,734
The pitch this lecture teaches sounds almost boring by comparison, and that's

6
00:00:21,734 --> 00:00:23,028
precisely why it lands.

7
00:00:23,028 --> 00:00:27,530
It leads with the testing architecture, the honest winner-loser-noise ratio, the

8
00:00:27,530 --> 00:00:31,919
fatigue watch, all the unglamorous discipline the last three sections actually

9
00:00:31,919 --> 00:00:32,257
built.

10
00:00:32,257 --> 00:00:35,408
Nobody else selling creative work is pitching a process.

11
00:00:35,408 --> 00:00:38,109
Most are pitching luck and calling it expertise.

12
00:00:38,109 --> 00:00:42,668
Sam's redacted Harlan report from Section 9.5 is the actual proof this pitch runs

13
00:00:42,668 --> 00:00:44,975
on, not a script written in the abstract.

14
00:00:44,975 --> 00:00:49,589
A real testing architecture, real numbers, one clear winner against several honest

15
00:00:49,589 --> 00:00:50,377
noise results.

16
00:00:50,377 --> 00:00:52,740
That report is the pitch, almost unedited.

17
00:00:52,740 --> 00:00:55,273
Notice what "almost unedited" is doing there.

18
00:00:55,273 --> 00:01:00,000
Redacting Harlan's brand name and specific numbers protects the client relationship.

19
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:04,502
It doesn't soften a single honest line about how the account actually performed.

20
00:01:04,502 --> 00:01:08,947
The structure of the report, the ratio, the discipline, travels intact into the

21
00:01:08,947 --> 00:01:10,354
pitch exactly as written.

22
00:01:10,904 --> 00:01:14,714
Read the left column as a warning, not a rival strategy to borrow from.

23
00:01:14,714 --> 00:01:19,115
A prospect who's been pitched the left column before, and most have, recognises it

24
00:01:19,115 --> 00:01:21,369
instantly and has learned not to trust it.

25
00:01:21,369 --> 00:01:25,769
Leading with the right column signals something different: you know what a testing

26
00:01:25,769 --> 00:01:29,741
programme actually looks like, including the parts that aren't flattering.

27
00:01:29,741 --> 00:01:31,619
The third row is worth dwelling on.

28
00:01:31,619 --> 00:01:35,805
A promise about results is exactly what Section 8's whole tree exists to avoid

29
00:01:35,805 --> 00:01:40,259
making about your own account, and it's not honest to make one about someone else's

30
00:01:40,259 --> 00:01:40,635
either.

31
00:01:40,635 --> 00:01:44,821
A kill rule and a CAC ceiling, decided before any spend happens, is a credible

32
00:01:44,821 --> 00:01:45,411
commitment.

33
00:01:45,411 --> 00:01:49,060
A results guarantee is a marketing line no structure actually backs.

34
00:01:49,060 --> 00:01:51,314
The fourth row matters more than it looks.

35
00:01:51,314 --> 00:01:55,661
"AI-powered" describes almost every competitor by now and means nothing specific.

36
00:01:55,661 --> 00:01:59,847
Naming the actual artifacts, a brief system, a pre-flight checklist, a testing

37
00:01:59,847 --> 00:02:04,355
architecture, tells a prospect exactly what they're buying, which is the opposite of

38
00:02:04,355 --> 00:02:05,213
a vague promise.

39
00:02:05,213 --> 00:02:07,843
Run a quick test on this table before you use it.

40
00:02:07,843 --> 00:02:12,297
Read the left column aloud and notice how familiar it sounds, because you've likely

41
00:02:12,297 --> 00:02:14,873
heard some version of it pitched to you already.

42
00:02:14,873 --> 00:02:19,327
That recognition, in the room, does real work before you've said a word of your own

43
00:02:19,327 --> 00:02:19,649
pitch.

44
00:02:20,199 --> 00:02:23,787
Four parts, and notice the proof comes before the ask, not after.

45
00:02:23,787 --> 00:02:28,312
Leading with price invites a prospect to evaluate you against a number before they

46
00:02:28,312 --> 00:02:30,078
understand what the number buys.

47
00:02:30,078 --> 00:02:34,328
Leading with a real, honest report earns the right to name a price afterward.

48
00:02:34,328 --> 00:02:37,418
The problem statement in part one is doing careful work.

49
00:02:37,418 --> 00:02:41,999
It names the actual shift, cheap generation, expensive judgement, the same argument

50
00:02:41,999 --> 00:02:44,206
Section 1 opened this whole course with.

51
00:02:44,206 --> 00:02:48,732
A prospect who recognises that problem in their own account is already most of the

52
00:02:48,732 --> 00:02:51,050
way to trusting the system that solves it.

53
00:02:51,050 --> 00:02:55,189
Notice what's missing from all four parts: a single invented success story.

54
00:02:55,189 --> 00:02:59,769
Everything here traces back to a report you can actually produce, not a claim you'd

55
00:02:59,769 --> 00:03:01,591
have to defend under questioning.

56
00:03:01,591 --> 00:03:06,061
That's deliberate, and it's the entire reason this pitch survives scrutiny that a

57
00:03:06,061 --> 00:03:07,275
flashier one wouldn't.

58
00:03:07,275 --> 00:03:11,469
Practice all four parts out loud before you use them for real, not just read

59
00:03:11,469 --> 00:03:11,966
silently.

60
00:03:11,966 --> 00:03:16,326
A pitch that reads well on a page can still trip on "the honest ratio" or "kill

61
00:03:16,326 --> 00:03:20,576
rule" the first time you say them under pressure, and stumbling over your own

62
00:03:20,576 --> 00:03:25,211
system's vocabulary undercuts the exact credibility this structure is built to earn.

63
00:03:25,761 --> 00:03:29,329
One honest trade worth naming before you use this pitch for real.

64
00:03:29,329 --> 00:03:32,349
A guarantee is exciting to hear and impossible to keep.

65
00:03:32,349 --> 00:03:36,960
The first time a prospect's account produces mostly noise, which Section 8.2 already

66
00:03:36,960 --> 00:03:41,406
told you is the honest normal, a guaranteed-results pitch collapses under its own

67
00:03:41,406 --> 00:03:41,845
promise.

68
00:03:41,845 --> 00:03:45,468
This pitch never made that promise, so it has nothing to collapse.

69
00:03:45,468 --> 00:03:49,860
Would you rather win a pitch you can't actually deliver on, or a slightly harder

70
00:03:49,860 --> 00:03:53,098
pitch you can defend for as long as the relationship lasts?

71
00:03:53,098 --> 00:03:57,709
The honest ratio is a harder sell in the room and a much easier one to live with six

72
00:03:57,709 --> 00:04:02,211
months later, when the account's actual results have to match whatever you said at

73
00:04:02,211 --> 00:04:02,760
the start.

74
00:04:02,760 --> 00:04:07,371
Expect to lose some pitches to the exciting version of this conversation, especially

75
00:04:07,371 --> 00:04:07,865
early on.

76
00:04:07,865 --> 00:04:12,311
That's not a sign the honest pitch is wrong, it's the cost of refusing to promise

77
00:04:12,311 --> 00:04:13,683
something you can't keep.

78
00:04:13,683 --> 00:04:18,130
The clients this pitch does win are the ones worth having for six months, not the

79
00:04:18,130 --> 00:04:21,259
ones who leave the moment the first noise result arrives.

80
00:04:21,809 --> 00:04:26,127
Next lecture prices what you just pitched, and names the work worth turning down

81
00:04:26,127 --> 00:04:28,340
before it costs you more than it's worth.

82
00:04:28,340 --> 00:04:32,821
Before that, draft your own version of part one, the problem statement, in your own

83
00:04:32,821 --> 00:04:33,145
words.

84
00:04:33,145 --> 00:04:37,463
If you can't state it without borrowing this lecture's exact phrasing, you don't

85
00:04:37,463 --> 00:04:41,296
understand it well enough yet to pitch it convincingly to someone else.

86
00:04:41,296 --> 00:04:45,830
Write parts two and three as well, even in rough form, using your own account's real

87
00:04:45,830 --> 00:04:47,504
artifacts rather than Harlan's.

88
00:04:47,504 --> 00:04:52,038
A pitch you've actually drafted once, however roughly, is genuinely available to you

89
00:04:52,038 --> 00:04:53,927
the moment an opportunity shows up.

90
00:04:53,927 --> 00:04:57,922
One you've only heard described in a lecture isn't, no matter how well you

91
00:04:57,922 --> 00:04:59,541
understood it while listening.
